  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the checkbox is markup you hand-write per item, or a JS renderer that creates checkboxes from data.

  - [1mPlain markup[0m — one [38;5;153m<input type="checkbox">[39m plus a [38;5;153m<label>[39m inside [38;5;153m<main>[39m. Native element already holds on/off state,
    so zero JS. Costs nothing to add a second item (copy two lines). Rules nothing out: if you later want a JS renderer,
    it emits this same markup.
  - [1mJS component[0m — a [38;5;153mrenderCheckbox(item)[39m function plus a JS file and a mount point. Buys you list-from-data rendering.
    Costs a script file and a rendering convention the rest of the page then has to follow. Harder to back out of than
    to add later.

  [1mRecommendation: plain markup.[0m You said basic and nothing fancy, and the native checkbox is literally the on/off state
  — a JS layer here would be state management around a thing that already manages its own state. Adding the renderer
  later is a clean upgrade; removing it isn't.

  [1mDesign (plain markup):[0m edit [38;5;153mindex.html[39m only. Add a [38;5;153m<ul>[39m in [38;5;153m<main>[39m with one task item: a checkbox [38;5;153m<input>[39m with an [38;5;153mid[39m,
  and a [38;5;153m<label for=...>[39m next to it so clicking the text toggles it too. Add a small [38;5;153m<style>[39m block with one rule —
  [38;5;153minput:checked + label[39m gets a line-through and grey text — so "done" is visible. No JS, no new files. Testing: open the
  file in a browser and toggle it; there's no test harness in this repo to add to.
